    "thm-finite-grid-conformal-risk-control": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-finite-nested-decision-family-and-bounded-risk",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $n,M\\ge1$, $B>0$, and $\\alpha\\in[0,B)$. Fix a deterministic strictly ordered grid $\\Lambda=\\{\\lambda_1<\\cdots<\\lambda_M\\}\\subset\\mathbb R$ and a probability space $(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)$. For $i=1,\\ldots,n+1$, let $W_i$ be a random element of $[0,B]^M$ with its Borel sigma-algebra, and write $L_i(\\lambda_j)=(W_i)_j$. The rows $W_1,\\ldots,W_{n+1}$ are **exchangeable** when their joint law is invariant under every permutation of the $n+1$ row indices. The coordinates $L_i(\\lambda_j)$ are the losses of the finite decision family at observation $i$; in a prediction problem they may be obtained by applying each fixed prediction rule to that observation and evaluating a specified loss.\n\nThe family is **loss-nested** when, with probability one, $L_i(\\lambda_1)\\ge\\cdots\\ge L_i(\\lambda_M)$ for every $i$. It is **endpoint-safe at level $\\alpha$** when, with probability one, $L_i(\\lambda_M)\\le\\alpha$ for every $i$. For any measurable selector $\\hat\\lambda$ taking values in $\\Lambda$ and determined by the calibration rows $W_1,\\ldots,W_n$, its **marginal expected test risk** is $\\mathbb E[L_{n+1}(\\hat\\lambda)]$, with expectation over all $n+1$ rows. This expectation is finite because every loss lies in $[0,B]$. Here “nested” means nonincreasing loss on the specified grid; literal inclusion of prediction sets is not required.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "alg-finite-grid-conformal-risk-control",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Input a deterministic ordered grid $\\Lambda=\\{\\lambda_1<\\cdots<\\lambda_M\\}$ with $M\\ge1$, a calibration size $n\\ge1$, a bound $B>0$, a target $\\alpha\\in[0,B)$, and calibration losses $L_i(\\lambda_j)\\in[0,B]$ for $1\\le i\\le n$ and $1\\le j\\le M$. Also supply the prediction rule $d_{\\lambda_j}$ associated with each grid point. For $j=1,\\ldots,M$ in order, compute\n$$T_j=\\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^n L_i(\\lambda_j)+B}{n+1}.$$\nReturn $(\\lambda_j,d_{\\lambda_j},\\text{pass})$ at the first index for which $T_j\\le\\alpha$. If no index passes, return $(\\lambda_M,d_{\\lambda_M},\\text{fallback})$. Monotonicity and the endpoint-safety assumption from the finite nested decision family are hypotheses for the risk theorem, not checks of the unobserved test loss.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-regular-conditional-distribution",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $X:(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,P)\\to(T,\\Tau)$ be a measurable random element and $\\mathcal G\\subseteq\\mathcal F$ a sub-sigma-algebra. A **regular conditional distribution of X given $\\mathcal G$** is a probability kernel $K:(\\Omega,\\mathcal G)\\to(T,\\Tau)$ satisfying\n$$\\int_H K(\\omega,A)\\,P(d\\omega)=P(H\\cap\\{X\\in A\\})\\qquad(H\\in\\mathcal G,\\ A\\in\\Tau).$$\n\n“Probability kernel” has the pointwise meaning of [[def-measure-kernel-and-probability-kernel]]: every $K(\\omega,\\cdot)$ is a probability measure, and every evaluation is $\\mathcal G$-measurable. Its evaluations lie in $[0,1]$ and are integrable. Thus the testing identity says exactly that $K(\\cdot,A)$ is a conditional-expectation version of $1_{\\{X\\in A\\}}$ in [[def-conditional-expectation-given-a-sigma-algebra]]. Conversely, a probability kernel with these version identities satisfies the displayed definition.\n\nThe kernel requirement holds at every sample point. Separate eventwise choices of conditional-expectation versions do not imply it and do not by themselves provide one exceptional set valid for all A. When A is empty the testing identity is zero on both sides, and when A=T it is P(H) on both sides. This defines a property of a supplied kernel and makes no existence or AC assertion.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-conditional-expectation-given-a-sigma-algebra",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,P)$ be a probability space, $\\mathcal G\\subseteq\\mathcal F$ a sub-sigma-algebra, and $X:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ integrable. A **conditional-expectation version of $X$ given $\\mathcal G$** is a real, $\\mathcal G$-measurable, integrable function $Y$ such that $\\int_A Y\\,dP=\\int_A X\\,dP$ for every $A\\in\\mathcal G$. No completeness of $\\mathcal G$ is assumed.\n\nExpectation means the integral of [[def-expectation-of-a-nonnegative-or-integrable-random-variable]]. Integrable representatives use [[def-l-one-of-a-measure]]; quotient notation is introduced after uniqueness.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-monotone-convergence-for-the-integral",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ be an arbitrary measure space. Let $f_n:X\\to[0,+\\infty]$ be measurable for every $n\\ge1$, with $0\\le f_1\\le f_2\\le\\cdots$, and suppose $f_n(x)\\uparrow f(x)$ for every $x\\in X$. Then\n$$\\int f_n\\,d\\mu\\uparrow\\int f\\,d\\mu.$$",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "cor-expectation-linearity-monotonicity-and-modulus-bound",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $X,Y$ be integrable real or complex random variables on one probability\nspace.\n\n1. For scalars $a,b$,\n   $$\\mathbb E[aX+bY]=a\\,\\mathbb E[X]+b\\,\\mathbb E[Y].$$\n2. If $X$ and $Y$ are real-valued and $X\\le Y$ almost surely, then\n   $$\\mathbb E[X]\\le\\mathbb E[Y].$$\n3. $$|\\mathbb E[X]|\\le\\mathbb E[|X|].$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.2",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "def-law-or-distribution-of-a-random-element",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $X:(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)\\to(S,\\Sigma)$ be a random element. Its\n**law** or **distribution** is the set function\n$$\\mathbb P_X:\\Sigma\\to[0,+\\infty],\\qquad \\mathbb P_X(B):=\\mathbb P(X^{-1}(B)).$$\n\nThus the law of $X$ records the probability of each measurable target set by\npulling it back to an event in the original probability space.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "def-expectation-of-a-nonnegative-or-integrable-random-variable",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)$ be a probability space.\n\n- If $X:\\Omega\\to[0,+\\infty]$ is measurable, its **expectation** is the\n  extended-valued integral\n  $$\\mathbb E[X]:=\\int_\\Omega X\\,d\\mathbb P\\in[0,+\\infty].$$\n- If $X:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ or $X:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb C$ is integrable in the sense\n  of [[def-integrable-real-and-complex-functions-and-their-integrals]], its\n  **expectation** is again\n  $$\\mathbb E[X]:=\\int_\\Omega X\\,d\\mathbb P,$$\n  now a finite real or complex number.\n\nFor an integrable real random variable,\n$$\\mathbb E[X]=\\mathbb E[X^+]-\\mathbb E[X^-],$$\nwhere $X^+$ and $X^-$ are its positive and negative parts.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derive-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "For any admissible input to the rule, every threshold is a finite real number since its numerator is a finite sum and $n+1>0$. The scan tests each index at most once, returning at the first passing index; if none passes, the specified fallback returns the supplied rule at index $M$. Since $M\\ge1$, both branches give a unique output after at most $M$ tests, without any monotonicity or endpoint-safety assumption.",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-1-2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "Write $C_j=(\\sum_{i=1}^nL_i(\\lambda_j)+B)/(n+1)$ and $S_j=(\\sum_{i=1}^{n+1}L_i(\\lambda_j))/(n+1)$. These are Borel functions of the finite row matrix. Each calibration pass event and the fallback event is a finite Boolean combination of Borel threshold sets, so the selected index is Borel-measurable. Define $j_*=\\min\\{j:S_j\\le\\alpha\\}$; this set is nonempty since $S_M\\le\\alpha$ by endpoint safety. Each event $\\{j_*=j\\}=\\{S_j\\le\\alpha\\}\\cap\\bigcap_{k<j}\\{S_k>\\alpha\\}$ is Borel, so $j_*$ and $g(W)=L_{n+1}(\\hat\\lambda(W))$ are Borel-measurable as well.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "If the calibration scan passes first at $q$, then $S_q=C_q+(L_{n+1}(\\lambda_q)-B)/(n+1)\\le C_q\\le\\alpha$, so $j_*\\le q=\\hat j$. If the scan has no pass, then $\\hat j=M$ and $j_*\\le M$. In either case $j_*\\le\\hat j$, and row monotonicity gives $L_{n+1}(\\lambda_{\\hat j})\\le L_{n+1}(\\lambda_{j_*})$ almost surely.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 1.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-2-2",
          "step": "2.2",
          "claim": "Set $V_i=L_i(\\lambda_{j_*})$. The full-row averages $S_j$ are invariant under row permutations, so $j_*$ is a symmetric measurable function of all rows. Consequently the vector $(V_1,\\ldots,V_{n+1})$ is exchangeable. Its entries have the same law by [F7]; since they are bounded, [F8] gives finite expectations and [F6] gives their equality. By [F6], the expectation of their average is $\\mathbb E[S_{j_*}]$, which is at most $\\alpha$ because $S_{j_*}\\le\\alpha$ almost surely. Thus $\\mathbb E[L_{n+1}(\\lambda_{j_*})]\\le\\alpha$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "F8",
            "step 1.2",
            "construct",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-3-1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "By step 2.1 and monotonicity of expectation in [F6], $\\mathbb E[L_{n+1}(\\hat\\lambda)]\\le\\mathbb E[L_{n+1}(\\lambda_{j_*})]$. Step 2.2 bounds the right side by $\\alpha$, proving the marginal guarantee.",
          "inputs": [
            "F6",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 2.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-4-1",
          "step": "4.1",
          "claim": "For almost every training outcome $\\omega$, apply the argument established in steps 1.2 and 2.1–2.2 under the conditional row law $Q_\\omega$ to obtain $\\int g\\,dQ_\\omega\\le\\alpha$, where $g(W)=L_{n+1}(\\hat\\lambda(W))$. The function is Borel-measurable by step 1.2 and bounded by $B$ by [F1]. Its dyadic lower approximations $g_m=2^{-m}\\lfloor2^m g\\rfloor$ are finite-valued Borel simple functions increasing to $g$. For each conditioning event $H\\in\\mathcal T$, decompose $g_m$ into its finitely many level-set indicators. The event identities in [F3] and linearity in [F6] then give $\\int_H\\int g_m\\,dQ_\\omega\\,d\\mathbb P(\\omega)=\\int_H g_m(W)\\,d\\mathbb P$. Applying [F5] to both sides gives the same identity for $g$. Each kernel integral of $g_m$ is $\\mathcal T$-measurable by [F3], so their pointwise limit $\\int g\\,dQ_\\omega$ is measurable. By [F4], this bounded function is a version of $\\mathbb E[g\\mid\\mathcal T]$. The sectionwise bound therefore gives the claimed conditional inequality almost surely.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "step 1.2",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 2.2",
            "step 3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "finite_smoke": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The hypotheses require n>=1 and a nonempty grid of size M>=1."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
