      ],
      "risk_review": {
        "status": "complete",
        "reviewer": "/root",
        "notes": "Reviewed steps 1.1–4.1 and current F1–F8 statements independently. Step 1.1 proves unique total finite-scan output and <=M tests for all admissible arrays, without risk assumptions. Borel threshold events give measurable calibration selector; endpoint safety ensures a full-row pass almost surely, with arbitrary fixed null-set extension. B correction gives S_q<=C_q, including fallback; monotonicity compares selected test losses. Symmetric full-row selector yields exchangeable bounded V_i, equal expectations by equality of laws/simple approximation, and mean <=alpha by finite linearity. Actual regular conditional law is assumed sectionwise exchangeable; bounded increasing dyadic simples, kernel event identities and MCT identify the conditional-expectation version. Checked M=1,n=1,alpha=0,ties,terminal equality,zero losses and no-pass fallback; no AC. Compared CRC §1.1 (4), §2.1 Theorem 1/proof and Foundations Ch.2 §2.1. The only refuter issue is the repaired placement of algorithm properties, not the risk proof. Consumers retain unchanged guarantee/hypotheses; no outside defect. Details in alpha-batch-98-3a report."
      }
    },
    "thm-finite-grid-monotone-envelope-risk-control": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-finite-nested-decision-family-and-bounded-risk",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $n,M\\ge1$, $B>0$, and $\\alpha\\in[0,B)$. Fix a deterministic strictly ordered grid $\\Lambda=\\{\\lambda_1<\\cdots<\\lambda_M\\}\\subset\\mathbb R$ and a probability space $(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)$. For $i=1,\\ldots,n+1$, let $W_i$ be a random element of $[0,B]^M$ with its Borel sigma-algebra, and write $L_i(\\lambda_j)=(W_i)_j$. The rows $W_1,\\ldots,W_{n+1}$ are **exchangeable** when their joint law is invariant under every permutation of the $n+1$ row indices. The coordinates $L_i(\\lambda_j)$ are the losses of the finite decision family at observation $i$; in a prediction problem they may be obtained by applying each fixed prediction rule to that observation and evaluating a specified loss.\n\nThe family is **loss-nested** when, with probability one, $L_i(\\lambda_1)\\ge\\cdots\\ge L_i(\\lambda_M)$ for every $i$. It is **endpoint-safe at level $\\alpha$** when, with probability one, $L_i(\\lambda_M)\\le\\alpha$ for every $i$. For any measurable selector $\\hat\\lambda$ taking values in $\\Lambda$ and determined by the calibration rows $W_1,\\ldots,W_n$, its **marginal expected test risk** is $\\mathbb E[L_{n+1}(\\hat\\lambda)]$, with expectation over all $n+1$ rows. This expectation is finite because every loss lies in $[0,B]$. Here “nested” means nonincreasing loss on the specified grid; literal inclusion of prediction sets is not required.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-finite-grid-conformal-risk-control",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every admissible input to the finite-grid risk-control rule, the rule returns a unique output after at most $M$ threshold tests; no loss monotonicity or endpoint-safety assumption is required for this assertion.\n\nLet $n,M\\ge1$, $B>0$, and $\\alpha\\in[0,B)$. On a probability space $(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)$, let $W_1,\\ldots,W_{n+1}$ be exchangeable random elements of $[0,B]^M$ with its Borel sigma-algebra, and write $L_i(\\lambda_j)=(W_i)_j$ on a deterministic grid $\\Lambda=\\{\\lambda_1<\\cdots<\\lambda_M\\}$. Assume that, almost surely, $L_i(\\lambda_1)\\ge\\cdots\\ge L_i(\\lambda_M)$ for every $i$ and $L_i(\\lambda_M)\\le\\alpha$ for every $i$. Apply the finite-grid risk-control rule to the first $n$ rows and let $\\hat\\lambda$ be its selected parameter. Then\n$$\\mathbb E[L_{n+1}(\\hat\\lambda)]\\le\\alpha,$$\nwhere the expectation is over all $n+1$ rows.\n\nFor a sub-sigma-algebra $\\mathcal T\\subseteq\\mathcal F$, suppose the full matrix $W=(W_1,\\ldots,W_{n+1})$, viewed in $([0,B]^M)^{n+1}$ with its product Borel sigma-algebra, has an actual regular conditional distribution $Q_\\omega$ given $\\mathcal T$. Suppose that, for almost every training outcome $\\omega$, the section $Q_\\omega$ is row-exchangeable and the same bounds, monotonicity, and endpoint hypotheses hold $Q_\\omega$-almost surely. Then the same argument gives $\\mathbb E[L_{n+1}(\\hat\\lambda)\\mid\\mathcal T]\\le\\alpha$ almost surely.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "cor-expectation-linearity-monotonicity-and-modulus-bound",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $X,Y$ be integrable real or complex random variables on one probability\nspace.\n\n1. For scalars $a,b$,\n   $$\\mathbb E[aX+bY]=a\\,\\mathbb E[X]+b\\,\\mathbb E[Y].$$\n2. If $X$ and $Y$ are real-valued and $X\\le Y$ almost surely, then\n   $$\\mathbb E[X]\\le\\mathbb E[Y].$$\n3. $$|\\mathbb E[X]|\\le\\mathbb E[|X|].$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-regular-conditional-distribution",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $X:(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,P)\\to(T,\\Tau)$ be a measurable random element and $\\mathcal G\\subseteq\\mathcal F$ a sub-sigma-algebra. A **regular conditional distribution of X given $\\mathcal G$** is a probability kernel $K:(\\Omega,\\mathcal G)\\to(T,\\Tau)$ satisfying\n$$\\int_H K(\\omega,A)\\,P(d\\omega)=P(H\\cap\\{X\\in A\\})\\qquad(H\\in\\mathcal G,\\ A\\in\\Tau).$$\n\n“Probability kernel” has the pointwise meaning of [[def-measure-kernel-and-probability-kernel]]: every $K(\\omega,\\cdot)$ is a probability measure, and every evaluation is $\\mathcal G$-measurable. Its evaluations lie in $[0,1]$ and are integrable. Thus the testing identity says exactly that $K(\\cdot,A)$ is a conditional-expectation version of $1_{\\{X\\in A\\}}$ in [[def-conditional-expectation-given-a-sigma-algebra]]. Conversely, a probability kernel with these version identities satisfies the displayed definition.\n\nThe kernel requirement holds at every sample point. Separate eventwise choices of conditional-expectation versions do not imply it and do not by themselves provide one exceptional set valid for all A. When A is empty the testing identity is zero on both sides, and when A=T it is P(H) on both sides. This defines a property of a supplied kernel and makes no existence or AC assertion.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-conditional-expectation-given-a-sigma-algebra",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,P)$ be a probability space, $\\mathcal G\\subseteq\\mathcal F$ a sub-sigma-algebra, and $X:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ integrable. A **conditional-expectation version of $X$ given $\\mathcal G$** is a real, $\\mathcal G$-measurable, integrable function $Y$ such that $\\int_A Y\\,dP=\\int_A X\\,dP$ for every $A\\in\\mathcal G$. No completeness of $\\mathcal G$ is assumed.\n\nExpectation means the integral of [[def-expectation-of-a-nonnegative-or-integrable-random-variable]]. Integrable representatives use [[def-l-one-of-a-measure]]; quotient notation is introduced after uniqueness.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-monotone-convergence-for-the-integral",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ be an arbitrary measure space. Let $f_n:X\\to[0,+\\infty]$ be measurable for every $n\\ge1$, with $0\\le f_1\\le f_2\\le\\cdots$, and suppose $f_n(x)\\uparrow f(x)$ for every $x\\in X$. Then\n$$\\int f_n\\,d\\mu\\uparrow\\int f\\,d\\mu.$$",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derive-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "For each $i$, $\\widetilde L_i(\\lambda_j)$ is a maximum of finitely many measurable coordinates, so it is measurable and lies in $[0,B]$. The set of indices in the maximum shrinks as $j$ increases, hence $\\widetilde L_i(\\lambda_{j+1})\\le\\widetilde L_i(\\lambda_j)$. At the endpoint, $\\widetilde L_i(\\lambda_M)=L_i(\\lambda_M)\\le\\alpha$. The same deterministic row map is applied to every $W_i$, and it commutes with row permutations; therefore the transformed rows remain exchangeable. Each calibration threshold is a finite sum of transformed coordinates, and each first-pass or fallback event is a finite Boolean combination of Borel threshold sets. Thus the selected parameter $\\widetilde\\lambda(W)$ and the losses $g(W)=L_{n+1}(\\widetilde\\lambda(W))$ and $h(W)=\\widetilde L_{n+1}(\\widetilde\\lambda(W))$ are Borel functions of the full matrix.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "construct",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-1-2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "By [F2], applying the calibration rule to the transformed rows gives $\\mathbb E[\\widetilde L_{n+1}(\\widetilde\\lambda)]\\le\\alpha$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "given"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "For every grid index $j$, the maximum defining $\\widetilde L_{n+1}(\\lambda_j)$ includes $L_{n+1}(\\lambda_j)$, so $L_{n+1}(\\widetilde\\lambda)\\le\\widetilde L_{n+1}(\\widetilde\\lambda)$ almost surely. Both variables are bounded and integrable; [F3] and step 1.2 give $\\mathbb E[L_{n+1}(\\widetilde\\lambda)]\\le\\alpha$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "step 1.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "For the conditional clause, let $Q_\\omega$ be the stated regular conditional distribution of the full matrix $W=(W_1,\\ldots,W_{n+1})$ given $\\mathcal T$. For almost every $\\omega$, its row law is exchangeable, its coordinates lie in $[0,B]$, and its endpoint losses are at most $\\alpha$ almost surely. Applying the same Borel tail-maximum map to each row preserves exchangeability under $Q_\\omega$, gives nonincreasing bounded rows, and leaves the endpoint unchanged. The marginal assertion of [F2], applied under each such probability measure $Q_\\omega$ to the transformed rows, gives $\\int h\\,dQ_\\omega\\le\\alpha$. Since $0\\le g\\le h\\le B$ pointwise, [F3] under $Q_\\omega$ yields $\\int g\\,dQ_\\omega\\le\\alpha$.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "For each $m\\ge1$, put $g_m=2^{-m}\\lfloor2^m g\\rfloor$. These are finite-valued Borel simple functions increasing pointwise to $g$. Write $g_m=\\sum_r c_{m,r}\\mathbf 1_{A_{m,r}}$ for Borel level sets $A_{m,r}$ in the full matrix space. By the kernel identity [F4], for every $H\\in\\mathcal T$, $$\\int_H\\int g_m\\,dQ_\\omega\\,d\\mathbb P(\\omega)=\\int_H g_m(W)\\,d\\mathbb P.$$ The kernel integrals of $g_m$ are $\\mathcal T$-measurable finite sums of kernel evaluations. By [F6], they increase to $k(\\omega)=\\int g\\,dQ_\\omega$, and the right-hand integrals increase to $\\int_H g(W)\\,d\\mathbb P$. Applying [F6] on both sides gives the same identity with $g$ in place of $g_m$. Thus $k$ is $\\mathcal T$-measurable, bounded, and has the defining integral identities of a conditional-expectation version by [F5]. Step 2.2 gives $k\\le\\alpha$ almost surely, proving the conditional bound.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.2",
            "construct"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "finite_smoke": [],
