          "reason": "With every calibration loss zero, proof1.1 computes the same threshold B divided by n plus one at every grid point. Passing at the first grid point therefore entails alpha at least this threshold, as the Example explicitly states.",
          "evidence": "Verification step 1.1 computes every corrected threshold as B/(n+1) because all calibration losses are zero. Therefore a first-grid-point pass requires alpha>=B/(n+1), which is the forward exactly-when direction in the Example.",
          "template_review": {
