    "ex-finite-grid-risk-control-fallback": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-finite-nested-decision-family-and-bounded-risk",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $n,M\\ge1$, $B>0$, and $\\alpha\\in[0,B)$. Fix a deterministic strictly ordered grid $\\Lambda=\\{\\lambda_1<\\cdots<\\lambda_M\\}\\subset\\mathbb R$ and a probability space $(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)$. For $i=1,\\ldots,n+1$, let $W_i$ be a random element of $[0,B]^M$ with its Borel sigma-algebra, and write $L_i(\\lambda_j)=(W_i)_j$. The rows $W_1,\\ldots,W_{n+1}$ are **exchangeable** when their joint law is invariant under every permutation of the $n+1$ row indices. The coordinates $L_i(\\lambda_j)$ are the losses of the finite decision family at observation $i$; in a prediction problem they may be obtained by applying each fixed prediction rule to that observation and evaluating a specified loss.\n\nThe family is **loss-nested** when, with probability one, $L_i(\\lambda_1)\\ge\\cdots\\ge L_i(\\lambda_M)$ for every $i$. It is **endpoint-safe at level $\\alpha$** when, with probability one, $L_i(\\lambda_M)\\le\\alpha$ for every $i$. For any measurable selector $\\hat\\lambda$ taking values in $\\Lambda$ and determined by the calibration rows $W_1,\\ldots,W_n$, its **marginal expected test risk** is $\\mathbb E[L_{n+1}(\\hat\\lambda)]$, with expectation over all $n+1$ rows. This expectation is finite because every loss lies in $[0,B]$. Here “nested” means nonincreasing loss on the specified grid; literal inclusion of prediction sets is not required.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "alg-finite-grid-conformal-risk-control",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Input a deterministic ordered grid $\\Lambda=\\{\\lambda_1<\\cdots<\\lambda_M\\}$ with $M\\ge1$, a calibration size $n\\ge1$, a bound $B>0$, a target $\\alpha\\in[0,B)$, and calibration losses $L_i(\\lambda_j)\\in[0,B]$ for $1\\le i\\le n$ and $1\\le j\\le M$. Also supply the prediction rule $d_{\\lambda_j}$ associated with each grid point. For $j=1,\\ldots,M$ in order, compute\n$$T_j=\\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^n L_i(\\lambda_j)+B}{n+1}.$$\nReturn $(\\lambda_j,d_{\\lambda_j},\\text{pass})$ at the first index for which $T_j\\le\\alpha$. If no index passes, return $(\\lambda_M,d_{\\lambda_M},\\text{fallback})$. Monotonicity and the endpoint-safety assumption from the finite nested decision family are hypotheses for the risk theorem, not checks of the unobserved test loss.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "cor-expectation-linearity-monotonicity-and-modulus-bound",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $X,Y$ be integrable real or complex random variables on one probability\nspace.\n\n1. For scalars $a,b$,\n   $$\\mathbb E[aX+bY]=a\\,\\mathbb E[X]+b\\,\\mathbb E[Y].$$\n2. If $X$ and $Y$ are real-valued and $X\\le Y$ almost surely, then\n   $$\\mathbb E[X]\\le\\mathbb E[Y].$$\n3. $$|\\mathbb E[X]|\\le\\mathbb E[|X|].$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derive-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Since every calibration loss is nonnegative, for each $j$ the algorithm's threshold satisfies $(\\sum_{i=1}^nL_i(\\lambda_j)+B)/(n+1)\\ge B/(n+1)>\\alpha$. No point passes, so the rule returns $\\lambda_M$.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "The endpoint condition gives $0\\le L_{n+1}(\\lambda_M)\\le\\alpha$ almost surely. The loss is bounded and integrable, so [F3] implies $\\mathbb E[L_{n+1}(\\lambda_M)]\\le\\alpha$.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "finite_smoke": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The example requires n>=1 and M>=1."
