    "cex-nonmonotone-finite-grid-risk-control": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-exchangeable-random-elements",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $Z_1,\\ldots,Z_n$ be random elements, $n\\ge1$, of one measurable space\n$(\\mathcal Z,\\mathscr Z)$. Their tuple $Z=(Z_1,\\ldots,Z_n)$ is a random element\nof $(\\mathcal Z^n,\\mathscr Z^{\\otimes n})$ by\n[[lem-coordinate-maps-and-permutations-are-measurable-on-finite-products]],\nand its law is the pushforward probability measure from\n[[def-law-or-distribution-of-a-random-element]]. For a permutation $\\pi$ of\n$\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}$, let $\\tau_\\pi(z)_i=z_{\\pi(i)}$. The tuple is\n**exchangeable** if\n$$\\mathbb P_Z\\circ\\tau_\\pi^{-1}=\\mathbb P_Z\\qquad\\text{for every permutation }\\pi.$$\n\nThis is invariance of the joint law, not an assertion of independence or identical marginal construction by independent sampling. The coordinate maps $\\tau_\\pi$ are measurable by the cited finite-product lemma.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "alg-finite-grid-conformal-risk-control",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Input a deterministic ordered grid $\\Lambda=\\{\\lambda_1<\\cdots<\\lambda_M\\}$ with $M\\ge1$, a calibration size $n\\ge1$, a bound $B>0$, a target $\\alpha\\in[0,B)$, and calibration losses $L_i(\\lambda_j)\\in[0,B]$ for $1\\le i\\le n$ and $1\\le j\\le M$. Also supply the prediction rule $d_{\\lambda_j}$ associated with each grid point. For $j=1,\\ldots,M$ in order, compute\n$$T_j=\\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^n L_i(\\lambda_j)+B}{n+1}.$$\nReturn $(\\lambda_j,d_{\\lambda_j},\\text{pass})$ at the first index for which $T_j\\le\\alpha$. If no index passes, return $(\\lambda_M,d_{\\lambda_M},\\text{fallback})$. Monotonicity and the endpoint-safety assumption from the finite nested decision family are hypotheses for the risk theorem, not checks of the unobserved test loss.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derive-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "By [A1] and [F1], the two loss rows are exchangeable. By [A2], their values are bounded by $B=1$, the endpoint is safe, and the family violates the required nonincreasing-loss condition on an event of probability $1/10$.",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "A2",
            "F1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-1-2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "If the calibration row is type $a$, the first threshold is $(0+1)/2=1/2\\le3/4$, so the algorithm selects $\\lambda_1$. If it is type $b$, the first threshold is $(1+1)/2=1>3/4$ and the second is $(3/4+1)/2=7/8>3/4$, so the algorithm falls back to $\\lambda_2$.",
          "inputs": [
            "A2",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "Conditional on a type-$a$ calibration row, independence makes the expected test loss at $\\lambda_1$ equal to $(1/10)0+(9/10)1=9/10$. Conditional on a type-$b$ calibration row, the selected endpoint loss is $3/4$ for either test type.",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "A2",
            "step 1.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-3-1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "Averaging over the calibration row gives $\\mathbb E[L_2(\\hat\\lambda)]=(1/10)(9/10)+(9/10)(3/4)=153/200=3/4+3/200>3/4=\\alpha$. Thus the asserted guarantee fails despite exchangeability, boundedness, and a safe endpoint.",
          "inputs": [
