    "rem-marginal-risk-control-and-adaptive-reuse": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-finite-grid-conformal-risk-control",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every admissible input to the finite-grid risk-control rule, the rule returns a unique output after at most $M$ threshold tests; no loss monotonicity or endpoint-safety assumption is required for this assertion.\n\nLet $n,M\\ge1$, $B>0$, and $\\alpha\\in[0,B)$. On a probability space $(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)$, let $W_1,\\ldots,W_{n+1}$ be exchangeable random elements of $[0,B]^M$ with its Borel sigma-algebra, and write $L_i(\\lambda_j)=(W_i)_j$ on a deterministic grid $\\Lambda=\\{\\lambda_1<\\cdots<\\lambda_M\\}$. Assume that, almost surely, $L_i(\\lambda_1)\\ge\\cdots\\ge L_i(\\lambda_M)$ for every $i$ and $L_i(\\lambda_M)\\le\\alpha$ for every $i$. Apply the finite-grid risk-control rule to the first $n$ rows and let $\\hat\\lambda$ be its selected parameter. Then\n$$\\mathbb E[L_{n+1}(\\hat\\lambda)]\\le\\alpha,$$\nwhere the expectation is over all $n+1$ rows.\n\nFor a sub-sigma-algebra $\\mathcal T\\subseteq\\mathcal F$, suppose the full matrix $W=(W_1,\\ldots,W_{n+1})$, viewed in $([0,B]^M)^{n+1}$ with its product Borel sigma-algebra, has an actual regular conditional distribution $Q_\\omega$ given $\\mathcal T$. Suppose that, for almost every training outcome $\\omega$, the section $Q_\\omega$ is row-exchangeable and the same bounds, monotonicity, and endpoint hypotheses hold $Q_\\omega$-almost surely. Then the same argument gives $\\mathbb E[L_{n+1}(\\hat\\lambda)\\mid\\mathcal T]\\le\\alpha$ almost surely.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-split-conformal-marginal-coverage",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $Z_i=(X_i,Y_i)$, $1\\le i\\le m+1$, be random elements of one measurable\nexample space $(\\mathcal Z,\\mathscr Z)$, and let $\\mathcal T$ be the training\nsigma-field. Suppose the tuple $Z=(Z_1,\\ldots,Z_{m+1})$ has a regular\nconditional distribution $Q_\\omega$ given $\\mathcal T$ in the sense of\n[[def-regular-conditional-distribution]], whose sections have joint laws\ninvariant under every coordinate permutation, as in\n[[def-exchangeable-random-elements]], for almost every $\\omega$. Let $H$ be a $\\mathcal T$-measurable\ntraining statistic, and let $s:\\mathcal H\\times\\mathcal Z\\to\\mathbb R$ be a\njointly measurable split score rule. Use the split set in\n[[alg-split-conformal-prediction-set]] at $0<\\alpha<1$. Then\n$$\\mathbb P\\{Y_{m+1}\\in C(X_{m+1})\\mid\\mathcal T\\}\\ge1-\\alpha\\quad\\text{almost surely}.$$\nIf the tie-randomized set $C_U$ is used with one $U$ of Lebesgue probability\nlaw on $[0,1]$ independent of $\\mathcal T\\vee\\sigma(Z)$, then\n$$\\mathbb P\\{Y_{m+1}\\in C_U(X_{m+1})\\mid\\mathcal T\\}=1-\\alpha\\quad\\text{almost surely}.$$\nIn either case, integration over the training sigma-field gives the same\nmarginal lower bound or exact marginal coverage.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-finite-grid-monotone-envelope-risk-control",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n,M\\ge1$, $B>0$, and $\\alpha\\in[0,B)$. On a probability space $(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)$, let $W_1,\\ldots,W_{n+1}$ be exchangeable random elements of $[0,B]^M$ with its Borel sigma-algebra, and write their coordinates as $L_i(\\lambda_j)$ on a deterministic grid $\\Lambda=\\{\\lambda_1<\\cdots<\\lambda_M\\}$; do not assume the original rows are nonincreasing. Assume $L_i(\\lambda_M)\\le\\alpha$ almost surely for every $i$. Define the finite tail-maximum losses\n$$\\widetilde L_i(\\lambda_j)=\\max_{j\\le k\\le M}L_i(\\lambda_k).$$\nApply the finite-grid risk-control rule to the transformed calibration rows and let $\\widetilde\\lambda$ be its selected parameter. Then\n$$\\mathbb E[L_{n+1}(\\widetilde\\lambda)]\\le\\alpha.$$\nThe expectation is over all calibration and test rows. If the rows have an actual regular conditional exchangeable law given a training sigma-algebra and the same bounds and endpoint condition hold almost surely in that law, the corresponding conditional expected-risk bound also holds almost surely.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-regular-conditional-distribution",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $X:(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,P)\\to(T,\\Tau)$ be a measurable random element and $\\mathcal G\\subseteq\\mathcal F$ a sub-sigma-algebra. A **regular conditional distribution of X given $\\mathcal G$** is a probability kernel $K:(\\Omega,\\mathcal G)\\to(T,\\Tau)$ satisfying\n$$\\int_H K(\\omega,A)\\,P(d\\omega)=P(H\\cap\\{X\\in A\\})\\qquad(H\\in\\mathcal G,\\ A\\in\\Tau).$$\n\n“Probability kernel” has the pointwise meaning of [[def-measure-kernel-and-probability-kernel]]: every $K(\\omega,\\cdot)$ is a probability measure, and every evaluation is $\\mathcal G$-measurable. Its evaluations lie in $[0,1]$ and are integrable. Thus the testing identity says exactly that $K(\\cdot,A)$ is a conditional-expectation version of $1_{\\{X\\in A\\}}$ in [[def-conditional-expectation-given-a-sigma-algebra]]. Conversely, a probability kernel with these version identities satisfies the displayed definition.\n\nThe kernel requirement holds at every sample point. Separate eventwise choices of conditional-expectation versions do not imply it and do not by themselves provide one exceptional set valid for all A. When A is empty the testing identity is zero on both sides, and when A=T it is P(H) on both sides. This defines a property of a supplied kernel and makes no existence or AC assertion.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-conditional-expectation-given-a-sigma-algebra",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,P)$ be a probability space, $\\mathcal G\\subseteq\\mathcal F$ a sub-sigma-algebra, and $X:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ integrable. A **conditional-expectation version of $X$ given $\\mathcal G$** is a real, $\\mathcal G$-measurable, integrable function $Y$ such that $\\int_A Y\\,dP=\\int_A X\\,dP$ for every $A\\in\\mathcal G$. No completeness of $\\mathcal G$ is assumed.\n\nExpectation means the integral of [[def-expectation-of-a-nonnegative-or-integrable-random-variable]]. Integrable representatives use [[def-l-one-of-a-measure]]; quotient notation is introduced after uniqueness.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-expectation-of-a-nonnegative-or-integrable-random-variable",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)$ be a probability space.\n\n- If $X:\\Omega\\to[0,+\\infty]$ is measurable, its **expectation** is the\n  extended-valued integral\n  $$\\mathbb E[X]:=\\int_\\Omega X\\,d\\mathbb P\\in[0,+\\infty].$$\n- If $X:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ or $X:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb C$ is integrable in the sense\n  of [[def-integrable-real-and-complex-functions-and-their-integrals]], its\n  **expectation** is again\n  $$\\mathbb E[X]:=\\int_\\Omega X\\,d\\mathbb P,$$\n  now a finite real or complex number.\n\nFor an integrable real random variable,\n$$\\mathbb E[X]=\\mathbb E[X^+]-\\mathbb E[X^-],$$\nwhere $X^+$ and $X^-$ are its positive and negative parts.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derive-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "The variable $X$ is $\\mathcal T$-measurable and integrable, so it is itself a version of $\\mathbb E[X\\mid\\mathcal T]$ by [F5]. Therefore $R=X$, $\\mathbb E R=(1/2)0+(1/2)(1/2)=1/4$, and $\\mathbb P(R>1/4)=1/2$. In particular, the chance constraint $\\mathbb P(R\\le1/4)\\ge1-\\delta$ fails for every $\\delta<1/2$.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-1-2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "[F1] gives a general marginal bound and a conditional bound only when its actual regular conditional law is sectionwise exchangeable and boundedness, loss nesting, and endpoint safety hold almost surely under those sections. Thus neither [F1] nor [F2] gives a general guarantee conditional on an arbitrary subgroup or feature value; [F2] concerns a particular coverage event under its own conditional-exchangeability hypotheses. An adaptive reuse or distribution shift that violates the applicable exchangeability hypotheses falls outside [F1] and [F2].",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "The conditional extension in [F1] requires the actual regular conditional law described by [F4]. The envelope in [F3] only dominates the original loss pointwise, so it may increase the selected decision's risk and make the resulting bound more conservative.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "F4"
          ]
        }
      ],
